    <div class="kicker" style="color:var(--ochre);">The journey</div>
    <h2 class="title">Follow the water, from source to tap</h2>
    <p class="lede" style="color:#3c392f;">Tap each stop for what's actually happening there.</p>

    <div class="journey-track">

      <div class="journey-stop open">
        <div class="journey-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M3 20 L9 8 L13 14 L16 9 L21 20 Z"/></svg></div>
        <div class="journey-header">
          <div class="journey-heading">
            <div class="journey-label">Gardens of Stone</div>
            <div class="journey-sub">Where the Coxs River begins</div>
          </div>
          <svg class="journey-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div class="journey-detail"><div class="journey-detail-inner">
          The Coxs River rises inside the Gardens of Stone landscape itself, at Gardiners Gap near Ben Bullen State Forest. This same country holds the headwaters of the Wolgan and Wollangambe rivers too, sandstone pagodas, hanging swamps and threatened species living alongside a landscape that's still largely unprotected from mining.
        </div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="journey-stop">
        <div class="journey-icon pollution"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M12 2v6M12 22v-6M4.9 4.9l4.2 4.2M14.9 14.9l4.2 4.2M2 12h6M16 12h6M4.9 19.1l4.2-4.2M14.9 9.1l4.2-4.2"/></svg></div>
        <div class="journey-header">
          <div class="journey-heading">
            <div class="journey-label">Coal mines, underneath the source</div>
            <div class="journey-sub">Extracting and discharging in the same landscape</div>
          </div>
          <svg class="journey-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div class="journey-detail"><div class="journey-detail-inner">
          Coal mines operating in this same landscape pump millions of litres of water out from deep underground every day, and discharge it back into the river, not all of it fully treated. During dry weather, mine water now makes up an estimated 80 to 90 per cent of this stretch's flow. That's not a river with some pollution in it, that's mine water wearing a river's name.
          <span class="journey-stat">80&ndash;90% of dry-weather flow is mine water</span>
        </div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="journey-stop">
        <div class="journey-icon pollution"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M12 3c3 4 6 7 6 11a6 6 0 0 1-12 0c0-4 3-7 6-11z"/></svg></div>
        <div class="journey-header">
          <div class="journey-heading">
            <div class="journey-label">Where the platypus disappeared</div>
            <div class="journey-sub">A river in trouble</div>
          </div>
          <svg class="journey-chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div class="journey-detail"><div class="journey-detail-inner">
          Independent university research has confirmed platypus have vanished entirely from stretches of the Coxs River near Lithgow, driven out by salinity and heavy metal pollution. Without healthy yabbies, mayflies and dragonflies, there's nothing left for a platypus to eat, and the same pollution has been shown to significantly reduce that invertebrate life.
        </div></div>
      </div>

